WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.229
Booting Linux from RAM is not one single feature.

2
00:00:03.229 --> 00:00:11.262
It is a family of designs where some part of the running system is backed by memory instead of ordinary persistent storage.

3
00:00:11.262 --> 00:00:22.209
In one design, a live image is copied into RAM during boot, so the system can run with less dependence on the USB drive, optical media, or network source that started it.

4
00:00:22.209 --> 00:00:30.084
In another design, the base root filesystem is read-only, and a writable tmpfs overlay records session changes in RAM.

5
00:00:30.084 --> 00:00:36.936
In another, the initramfs stages early boot work in memory before the final root filesystem is mounted.

6
00:00:36.936 --> 00:00:45.835
Some systems go further and make the whole root effectively volatile, so each reboot returns to a clean state. The appeal is practical.

7
00:00:45.835 --> 00:00:54.183
RAM-backed reads can be fast after the system is loaded. Disk writes can be reduced, which helps low-wear media and lab images.

8
00:00:54.183 --> 00:01:02.767
A live session can be portable. A kiosk, classroom, training machine, or recovery environment can reset cleanly after each boot.

9
00:01:02.767 --> 00:01:09.619
A test machine can be changed freely because the changes disappear unless they are deliberately saved.

10
00:01:09.619 --> 00:01:19.227
The warning is just as important: RAM is temporary. If the system reboots, loses power, crashes, or runs out of memory, RAM-only changes are gone.

11
00:01:19.227 --> 00:01:29.465
That can be a feature when the goal is clean rollback, but it can be a disaster if the learner expected logs, configuration, updates, or user data to persist.

12
00:01:29.465 --> 00:01:38.837
Good RAM-boot design starts with a clear goal. Are you trying to remove dependence on the boot device after startup? Reduce writes to storage?

13
00:01:38.837 --> 00:01:47.815
Build a disposable lab workstation? Keep a read-only base image with a temporary writable layer? Test software in a clean environment?

14
00:01:47.815 --> 00:01:57.817
Each goal points to a different pattern and a different persistence plan. Avoid treating RAM booting as automatically faster, safer, or more private.

15
00:01:57.817 --> 00:02:05.613
It is an engineering choice with tradeoffs that must match the workload, hardware, distribution, and recovery plan.
